eed ensure full statement directly supported. Statement could be: \"As of a 2016 Forbes Africa feature, Mtukudzi had made 67 albums.\" Evidence: W3. But careful: \"67 albums later\" might mean 67 albums later than his first recording? It could mean he had released 67 albums by then. It says \"67 albums later, Mtukudzi still spoke...\" immediately followed by Forbes Africa in 2016. So yes.\n\nCould also extract: In 2016, Mtukudzi told Forbes Africa, \"I am yet to decide on a career...\" That is a quote about his career. But OneGap is about date and album count. Maybe include as part? Need not enumerate general webpage facts. The first finding about date and albums is enough. But schema max 3. Maybe separate findings? \"Oliver Mtukudzi was featured/telling Forbes Africa in 2016\" and \"He had 67 albums by that time.\" They are related. Could produce one finding containing both, or two. The instructions: extract NEW factual findings. OneGap target: exact date/month and number of albums. Since exact date/month not known, only year 2016. Statement could be: \"A Forbes Africa feature in which Mtukudzi said he was yet to decide on a career appeared in 2016, by which point he had 67 albums.\" That is directly supported? It says \"67 albums later, Mtukudzi still spoke ... telling Forbes Africa in 2016...\" So yes. But \"by which point\" maybe infer. Better: \"In a 2016 Forbes Africa feature, Mtukudzi said he was yet to decide on a career; the article notes 67 albums later.\" Hmm.\n\nNeed maybe no findings? Let's consider if observation does not establish exact date/month nor album count at feature? It says \"67 albums later\" right before quote, so yes number of albums by 2016. The OneGap: exact date/month and number of albums Mtukudzi had by that feature are not established. The observation establishes year 2016 and 67 albums. However \"exact date/month\" still not established; only year. But it materially advances by providing year and album count. This should be finding.\n\nNeed include evidence_refs W3.\n\nCould include statement: \"In 2016, Forbes Africa featured Mtukudzi; by that point, he had 67 albums.\" Is \"by that point\" supported? \"67 albums later, Mtukudzi still spoke ... telling Forbes Africa in 2016\" The 67 albums later likely at the time of the quote. I'd phrase exactly: \"The Forbes Africa feature in which Mtukudzi said he was still deciding on a career is dated to 2016; the same passage states this was 67 albums later.\" That preserves exact wording and avoids overclaim. But the OneGap asks number of albums Mtukudzi had by that feature. The statement \"the same passage states this was 67 albums later\" might not directly say he had 67 albums by feature, but close. To be useful: \"By the time of a 2016 Forbes Africa feature, Mtukudzi had 67 albums.\" Is that directly supported? The sentence \"67 albums later...\" means after 67 albums, and then quote. I think yes.\n\nCould also include: \"The Forbes Africa feature was published in 2016 (not exact month/day).\" But no need mention not exact month.\n\nNeed output JSON with findings array. Only one finding maybe. But maybe we can split into two:\n1. \"Oliver Mtukudzi told Forbes Africa in 2016 that he was yet to decide on a career...\" (date year)\n2. \"By the time of that Forbes Africa feature, Mtukudzi had 67 albums.\" (number)\nBoth evidence W3. But the instruction says findings should add facts not already represented in C. We don't know C. Maybe existing C already has date? It says OneGap not established, so likely C lacks. Two findings might be okay. But max 3. However if split, the second \"by the time\" is inferential. The first direct. Could combine.\n\nPotential issue: \"exact date/month\" OneGap wants exact date/month; observation only gives 2016. Should we create finding saying \"The Forbes Africa feature was in 2016.\" That materially advances, though not exact. It's factual. It adds year. The statement \"in 2016\" is directly supported. The number \"67 albums\" is directly supported. So finding: \"Mtukudzi's Forbes Africa feature in which he discussed still deciding on a career was in 2016, and the article states he had 67 albums by then.\" This may be two facts in one statement.\n\nNeed maybe include quote? It says \"I am yet to decide on a career...\" Could be relevant to Forbes feature. But OneGap only date and albums. The quote might be additional but not needed. The instruction: do not enumerate general webpage facts. So keep focused.\n\nNeed final JSON only, no markdown. Ensure valid JSON.\n\nLet's craft:\n{\"findings\":[{\"statement\":\"The Forbes Africa feature in which Oliver Mtukudzi said he was yet to decide on a career is dated to 2016, and the same account places it at 67 albums into his career.\",\"evidence_refs\":[\"W3\"]}]}\n\nIs \"places it at 67 albums into his career\" exactly? It says \"67 albums later, Mtukudzi still spoke...\" yes.\n\nMaybe \"by that feature\" not \"same account\". The source text: \"67 albums later, Mtukudzi still spoke as if he was in search of what to call a career, telling Forbes Africa in 2016...\" So \"67 albums later\" means after 67 albums? Could be 67 albums later than something? It doesn't specify from when, but likely from start. It clearly indicates 67 albums.\n\nShould we include the quote? The statement can be: \"In 2016, Forbes Africa published a feature in which Mtukudzi said he was yet to decide on a career; the article notes that this was 67 albums later.\" That's directly supported. It does not say \"he had 67 albums\" but implies. Maybe OneGap wants number. \"67 albums later\" is the number. The finding: \"The 2016 Forbes Africa feature came after 67 albums, according to the article.\" That's precise. But \"came after 67 albums\" is a bit odd. Better: \"The article states that Mtukudzi had 67 albums at the time of the 2016 Forbes Africa feature.\" Directly supported? I think yes.\n\nLet's ensure no unsupported exact date. We won't say exact date. It's fine.\n\nCould there be another finding: \"Mtukudzi was born in September 1952 in Highfield\" unrelated. Not OneGap. No.\n\nCould there be another relevant: \"Mtukudzi joined Wagon Wheel band in 1977 alongside Thomas Mapfumo\" unrelated. No.\n\nCould there be \"Pakare Paye located in Norton about 45 km from Harare\" unrelated. No.\n\nThus one finding.\n\nBut the prompt says \"Extract 0–3 NEW factual findings across this acquisition only when the observations directly support the full statement, materially advance this OneGap, and add facts not already adequately represented in C.\" If C already has 2016 Forbes Africa feature? OneGap says exact date/month and number of albums not established. It might already have \"Forbes Africa in 2016\" in C? The OneGap says exact date/month of the Forbes Africa feature in the 2010s and number of albums not established. It implies maybe the 2010s but not exact date. It doesn't say year unknown. If C already has year 2016, then our year finding duplicates. But OneGap phrasing \"exact date/month of the Forbes Africa feature in the 2010s\" suggests we know it's in 2010s but not exact date/month. Maybe C already has 2016? Hmm. Existing C not supplied, but OneGap says \"exact date/month of the Forbes Africa feature in the 2010s and the number of albums Mtukudzi had by that feature are not established.\" That phrasing could mean C already has \"Forbes Africa feature in the 2010s\" maybe not year. It says exact date/month, not year. If C already had 2016, then year not new. But we don't know. We need add facts not already adequately represented. The observation provides year 2016 and 67 albums. The 67 albums is definitely new per OneGap. The year might be new if C lacked. But to avoid duplicate, we can focus on number of albums, and maybe mention 2016 as context. The statement \"Mtukudzi had 67 albums by the time of the Forbes Africa feature in 2016.\" If C already has 2016, the new part is 67. That's fine; statement includes both but adds num